WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:15.548
The Florida reef tract is the US's

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.617
only barrier-reef system.

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.586
It's about 300 miles long,

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:22.589
and what we saw over the last few decades

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:26.292
is an incredible decline in coral cover.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.294
What we're looking at
is the potential loss

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:30.063
of an entire ecosystem,

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:33.366
which we've never
experienced in human history,

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.468
and I don't think anyone wants to find out

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:38.772
what that would mean if
we had a complete collapse

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:41.374
of our coral-reef ecosystems.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.609
My name is Jessica Levy,

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:45.378
and I am the director
of restoration strategy

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.614
for the Coral Restoration Foundation.

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:50.717
The Coral Restoration
Foundation is a not-for-profit

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:53.086
headquartered in the Florida Keys.

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:56.423
So, here we are in one of
Coral Restoration Foundation’s

00:00:56.423 --> 00:00:58.091
offshore coral nurseries.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:00.427
It's about an acre and a half wide.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:03.530
It's the largest offshore
coral nursery in the world.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.666
And it's a spectacular site to visit.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:10.336
The restoration process starts,
in the very original sense,

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:13.239
with collections of natural corals.

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:16.810
These are corals that are
at risk to some event,

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.546
so we actually go out to our local reefs

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:21.781
and harvest from them,

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:25.251
and instead of return visits to the wild,

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.054
we use that coral in a nursery system

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:29.923
where that original collection is allowed

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:33.860
to grow over time. And as it grows,

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.097
you take subsequent cuttings
from that original collection

00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:40.366
and you produce more and more corals.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:43.269
The main structure that
CRF uses and pioneered

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.605
is what's known as the coral tree.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:47.907
It's a very simple structure

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.176
that floats in the water column.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.445
It's made of PVC and fiberglass.

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.746
In our nurseries in particular,

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:57.417
we have upwards of 500 of
these coral structures,

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.919
and the corals are actually suspended

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.155
on those structures themselves.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:03.723
So you'll see our divers swimming

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.092
through this forest of coral trees.

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.727
Some of the main activities

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.096
that they're likely to do in a nursery

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:13.700
is cleaning, removing algae and biofouls

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.137
and any other organisms that
have grown on those structures

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:20.874
and might compete with that
coral for living space.

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:24.611
We use very simple materials
to work on our trees.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:27.380
We use wire brushes or a chisel

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.349
to clean the structures themselves.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.218
We use clippers to actually cut the coral

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.420
or cut the monofilament line

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.357
that the coral is suspended
from the tree with.

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.492
While you can work pretty heavy-handed

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:43.429
with the trees themselves,
corals are a living animal,

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:46.332
it's a very thin layer of animal tissue,

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.500
so we're very gentle

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.336
when we're working with the coral itself.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:53.173
Typically, when we
suspend a coral fragment,

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:56.976
it's maybe a couple of
centimeters long or wide,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.512
and then after about nine months,

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:03.283
it'll grow to about
the size of a football,

00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.919
at which point that is
an appropriate size,

00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:07.353
what we call reef-ready,

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:10.690
and able to be moved to a
nearby restoration site.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.494
We will go to a structure,
go to a coral tree,

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.530
and clip from those
individual corals there.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.499
This takes advantage of a process

00:03:19.499 --> 00:03:21.768
known as asexual fragmentation,

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:25.104
essentially making clones of
your original, your individual.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:28.074
And we fragment from
the corals on the trees.

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:30.910
We'll move them to either new trees

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.912
and resuspend them to continue to grow

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:35.315
and take advantage of that nursery phase,

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:38.585
or we'll fragment from the existing stock

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:40.653
and go through a process of harvesting.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.590
So we'll go to these trees themselves,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.693
and we'll actually collect
corals from those nurseries.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.995
Those corals are then
moved onto our boats,

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:50.930
and they sit in seawater,

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:54.200
and then they'll be transported
to a nearby restoration site

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.769
to begin the process of outplanting.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.938
Once we're at an outplant site,

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.940
our divers enter the water.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.310
Someone from the boat hands
them crates of corals.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.380
Those divers then swim down.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.650
Once we're at the reef, the
first step in the process

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.186
is to find your outplant site.

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:17.056
So this is an area that's
relatively hard bottom,

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:21.561
open, lots of space, and
available substrate to work with.

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:25.365
A diver then clears the reef area

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:28.468
where that coral is going
to eventually be secured.

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:31.771
What we look for is white, crisp rock.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:34.774
It's basically limestone or old coral.

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.043
We then mix our two-part epoxy

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.479
in this almost, like, pulling-taffy motion

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:43.483
until it's one single clay or putty.

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.818
We'll take a small pinch of that epoxy

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.186
that we've just mixed,

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:50.089
push that into the reef substrate itself,

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.225
and then gently place the coral

00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:54.360
on top of that point that we've cleared.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.862
And that epoxy will harden

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:58.197
and have attached that
coral to the reef itself.

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:01.567
We'll then pretty much
step and repeat the process

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.370
until an individual diver
has gotten 100 corals down

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.206
or until a team of divers
has gotten anywhere

00:05:07.206 --> 00:05:11.377
from 300 to 800 corals
done in a single day.

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.680
These corals are placed in clusters

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.382
of about 50 to 70 individuals.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:19.319
They're placed in clusters
of the same genetic material,

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:21.954
so they're essentially
clones of their neighbors.

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:25.692
Over time, these corals
start to branch out

00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:27.327
and they start to touch their neighbors,

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:28.928
and they start to fuse,

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:32.832
and what happens is they
create this one large thicket.

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:35.168
So this thicket has all these in and outs

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:39.038
that allow small fish to duck
in and hide from predators.

00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:41.441
It allows for a lot of invertebrates

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:42.942
to thrive there as well.

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:46.479
This is the structure that
we really want to produce.

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:48.214
The important part is promoting

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:51.651
that genetic diversity on the reef site.

00:05:51.651 --> 00:05:54.153
Our nursery program on an annual basis

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:55.688
produces probably about

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:59.826
40,000 reef-ready corals.

00:05:59.826 --> 00:06:04.330
The reality is, is restoration
is never a one-off.

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:05.531
It's not something you do

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:08.801
and then pretty much ever walk away from.

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:11.204
There probably will always be work to do,

00:06:11.204 --> 00:06:15.441
and the reason is, is it's
because we have not removed

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:20.380
all the pressures and the stress
that our reefs face today.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.750
And until we fully remove
all these pressures,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.452
we're still going to have
the need for restoration.

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:28.354
Restoration right now kind of works

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:30.890
to keep pace with that loss,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.894
to keep pace with an
ecosystem still kind of going,

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.430
even if it's maybe considered
to be treading water.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:39.832
It's still about keeping
the system alive,

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:42.335
because if we don't do anything now,

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:45.204
there's not going to be
anything left to work with.

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:47.507
This is not just a Florida problem.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:49.275
Reefs around the world are facing

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:53.880
the exact same challenges
and scale of decline.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.415
In the last 30 years,

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:59.719
global coral reefs have declined by 50%.

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:04.023
This has a cascading effect
on a lot of different aspects.

00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:06.993
For example, 25% of marine life

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:09.028
depends on a coral-reef system

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:11.230
at some point in their life history,

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.065
whether they live there full-time

00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:16.636
or they migrate to it some
point in their life cycle.

00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:19.906
Additionally, coral reefs are a part of

00:07:19.906 --> 00:07:21.574
a healthy ocean system.

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:25.411
About 70% of the oxygen
that we as humans breathe

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:27.980
comes from our oceans.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:31.751
Florida, in particular,
sits at about 98% decline,

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:35.021
which means about 2% coral cover left,

00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:37.290
which, although drastic and terrible,

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.559
it's better than zero.

00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:43.429
We don't want to find out
what happens when we hit zero.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:46.065
It's easy to talk about the impact

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:49.669
of a degraded coral reef
to a local community

00:07:49.669 --> 00:07:51.871
that has a very one-to-one connection.

00:07:51.871 --> 00:07:53.840
The reality is it affects all of us,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.343
whether you live on a
coastline or you live somewhere

00:07:57.343 --> 00:08:00.880
where you've never even seen
the ocean to begin with.

